1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,750
A person with severe paralysis
thinks four words.

2
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:05,250
They don't say them.
Nothing leaves their mouth.

3
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:07,100
Chitty chitty bang bang.

4
00:00:07,450 --> 00:00:11,300
A computer connected to electrodes in their
brain recognizes the phrase more than

5
00:00:11,320 --> 00:00:15,960
98 percent of the time. Then it starts
turning their silent sentences into text on a

6
00:00:15,980 --> 00:00:20,290
screen. That was a real experiment
at Stanford, published in 2025.

7
00:00:20,680 --> 00:00:22,770
And the strange part isn't that it worked.

8
00:00:22,790 --> 00:00:24,180
It's what the computer had to read.

9
00:00:24,200 --> 00:00:27,590
A voice that makes no
sound. You have one too.

10
00:00:27,610 --> 00:00:30,110
Say this in your head: I
should call him tomorrow.

11
00:00:30,130 --> 00:00:32,640
Look at what just happened.
Your lips didn't move.

12
00:00:32,660 --> 00:00:37,760
Nothing vibrated in the air. And yet a
sentence played inside you, words in order,

13
00:00:37,780 --> 00:00:41,780
maybe even in a tone, in something
that felt exactly like a voice.

14
00:00:41,800 --> 00:00:44,030
So what did that computer actually read?

15
00:00:44,050 --> 00:00:48,060
What is a voice with no sound? And if
it can be found, why do some people say

16
00:00:48,080 --> 00:00:51,460
they've never had one? The obvious
answer is the one you already believe.

17
00:00:51,480 --> 00:00:54,480
That voice is just what
thinking sounds like.

18
00:00:54,500 --> 00:00:59,000
Thought is made of words, the words come
with a voice, and everyone's is running all

19
00:00:59,020 --> 00:01:00,610
day, the way yours is.

20
00:01:00,680 --> 00:01:03,010
Then researchers tried
to catch it in the act.

21
00:01:03,030 --> 00:01:07,280
In studies by Russell Hurlburt and Chris
Heavey, volunteers carried a beeper.

22
00:01:07,300 --> 00:01:11,420
At random moments it went off, and they
had to freeze and record what was in their

23
00:01:11,440 --> 00:01:14,920
mind at that exact instant.
Not what they usually think.

24
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:19,360
What was there. In one influential study,
inner speech showed up in about a quarter

25
00:01:19,380 --> 00:01:20,620
of those moments.

26
00:01:21,060 --> 00:01:23,690
Twenty-six percent. And the
average hides what matters.

27
00:01:23,710 --> 00:01:27,540
Some people never reported it. Others
reported it in roughly three out of four

28
00:01:27,560 --> 00:01:29,570
moments. Be careful with that number.

29
00:01:29,700 --> 00:01:32,590
It doesn't mean humans think in
words a quarter of the time.

30
00:01:32,610 --> 00:01:37,030
It's what about 30 people reported at
sampled moments, using one method.

31
00:01:37,120 --> 00:01:40,050
But it breaks the picture of
a narrator that never stops.

32
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:42,530
Your instinct is already pushing back.

33
00:01:42,550 --> 00:01:46,620
I talk to myself constantly. But there's
a reason your instinct and the data can

34
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:51,450
disagree. When researchers compared the two
ways of asking, questionnaires, the "how

35
00:01:51,470 --> 00:01:56,260
often do you talk to yourself" kind, gave
higher estimates than random moments did.

36
00:01:56,280 --> 00:02:00,540
What you remember about your mind and what
was in it at one random second are two

37
00:02:00,560 --> 00:02:03,630
different measurements. So
what filled the other moments?

38
00:02:03,980 --> 00:02:08,560
Pictures. A feeling. Awareness of
the room, the chair, the light.

39
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:13,260
And one report that's harder to picture:
a specific thought, with no words and no

40
00:02:13,280 --> 00:02:18,080
image attached. That one is still debated,
so treat it as a report, not a verdict.

41
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:22,880
But the pattern holds. A mind without
a constant voice is not an empty mind.

42
00:02:22,900 --> 00:02:25,950
Picture two people sitting
silently in the same room.

43
00:02:25,980 --> 00:02:28,170
One is narrating. Why did I say that?

44
00:02:28,190 --> 00:02:29,650
I need to leave in five minutes.

45
00:02:29,800 --> 00:02:33,920
The other is moving through images
and sensations, with hardly a word.

46
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:36,420
Neither suspects the other's
mind works differently.

47
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:39,540
And even for people who do hear
a voice, it isn't one thing.

48
00:02:39,560 --> 00:02:41,280
Sometimes it's a
conversation with yourself.

49
00:02:41,300 --> 00:02:44,870
Sometimes it's a single word
that carries a whole thought.

50
00:02:44,890 --> 00:02:46,400
Which makes the question sharper.

51
00:02:46,420 --> 00:02:49,820
If thinking can happen without it,
why does the voice exist at all?

52
00:02:49,840 --> 00:02:52,620
Watch a two- or three-year-old
working through a problem.

53
00:02:52,640 --> 00:02:55,910
They talk out loud, to
nobody, steering themselves.

54
00:02:55,930 --> 00:02:58,010
Researchers call it private speech.

55
00:02:58,030 --> 00:03:00,200
As children grow, it gets quieter.

56
00:03:00,220 --> 00:03:04,280
Then it goes silent. One influential idea
says that's where your inner voice comes

57
00:03:04,300 --> 00:03:05,240
from.

58
00:03:05,260 --> 00:03:09,180
Language, which starts as a line between
you and other people, gets aimed back at

59
00:03:09,200 --> 00:03:12,890
yourself, then folded inward.
There's real evidence behind it.

60
00:03:12,910 --> 00:03:16,890
But it explains how the voice could
appear, not how it definitely did.

61
00:03:16,910 --> 00:03:20,680
Later work has made the picture messier,
with different kinds of inner speech that

62
00:03:20,700 --> 00:03:23,990
may not share one origin. Which
brings us back to the computer.

63
00:03:24,010 --> 00:03:25,750
It wasn't listening to the ears.

64
00:03:25,770 --> 00:03:27,380
It wasn't listening to the throat.

65
00:03:27,400 --> 00:03:31,420
It was listening to the motor cortex, a
part of the brain involved in controlling

66
00:03:31,440 --> 00:03:35,960
speech. In four participants, the Stanford
team found that imagining a sentence

67
00:03:35,980 --> 00:03:38,530
produced a pattern similar
to trying to say it.

68
00:03:38,550 --> 00:03:41,840
Weaker, but close, and
different enough to tell apart.

69
00:03:41,860 --> 00:03:47,080
From that, the computer decoded imagined
sentences in real time, with up to 74

70
00:03:47,100 --> 00:03:52,040
percent accuracy, from a
vocabulary of 125,000 words.

71
00:03:52,060 --> 00:03:56,340
It even caught numbers participants were
silently counting, without being asked.

72
00:03:56,360 --> 00:03:59,450
That last detail is part of why
the team built the password.

73
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:02,550
The system only translates after
the user chooses to start.

74
00:04:02,570 --> 00:04:07,800
This is not mind reading. It's four people,
implanted electrodes, controlled tasks,

75
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:11,540
and a lock the user holds. What the
computer found wasn't a secret thought.

76
00:04:11,560 --> 00:04:13,820
It was speech with the sound taken out.

77
00:04:13,840 --> 00:04:18,040
So is the inner voice just your brain
preparing to speak, and stopping short?

78
00:04:18,060 --> 00:04:18,980
Not quite.

79
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:21,350
Two things argue against
that simple picture.

80
00:04:21,400 --> 00:04:26,020
First, some people with aphasia, who struggle
to produce speech aloud, keep aspects

81
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:30,520
of their inner speech. Aphasia varies widely
and inner speech is hard to measure, so

82
00:04:30,540 --> 00:04:35,380
that's a clue, not a proof. But it suggests
inner speech is related to speaking, yet

83
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:38,190
isn't simply speaking with
the volume turned down.

84
00:04:38,540 --> 00:04:42,470
Second, silent speech seems to carry
a forecast of how it would sound.

85
00:04:42,490 --> 00:04:46,820
When your brain sends a command to move,
it keeps an internal copy and uses it to

86
00:04:46,840 --> 00:04:49,730
predict the result. It's why
you can't tickle yourself.

87
00:04:49,750 --> 00:04:50,850
In 2017,

88
00:04:51,300 --> 00:04:55,310
Thomas Whitford and his colleagues tested
whether silent speech does the same.

89
00:04:55,680 --> 00:04:57,590
Participants wore EEG caps.

90
00:04:57,660 --> 00:05:02,510
At a precisely timed moment, they silently
imagined saying a single syllable, like

91
00:05:02,530 --> 00:05:05,930
"ba," while a real syllable
played through headphones.

92
00:05:05,950 --> 00:05:09,880
When the imagined syllable matched the real
one, the brain's early response to the

93
00:05:09,900 --> 00:05:12,950
sound got weaker. When it
didn't match, it didn't.

94
00:05:12,970 --> 00:05:17,680
Imagining a sound, in complete silence,
changed how the brain answered the real

95
00:05:17,700 --> 00:05:20,230
thing, but only if it was the right sound.

96
00:05:20,250 --> 00:05:24,240
So when you say a sentence in your head,
your brain appears to forecast how it would

97
00:05:24,260 --> 00:05:27,780
sound, in detail fine enough to
tell one syllable from another.

98
00:05:27,800 --> 00:05:33,140
One leading idea says that same forecast
helps the brain tag the voice as yours.

99
00:05:33,170 --> 00:05:35,410
That's a model, not a settled fact.

100
00:05:35,430 --> 00:05:39,560
And keep the limits straight. The experiment
measured a brain response, not what

101
00:05:39,580 --> 00:05:44,700
anyone consciously heard. A 2023 analysis
of imaging studies also suggests that

102
00:05:44,720 --> 00:05:49,280
different kinds of inner speech lean on
speech production, on simulating how speech

103
00:05:49,300 --> 00:05:51,010
sounds, or on both.

104
00:05:51,220 --> 00:05:54,700
So the forecast is a strong clue
to the voice, not the whole of it.

105
00:05:54,720 --> 00:05:59,130
Still, a voice built from borrowed
machinery could just be a side effect.

106
00:05:59,150 --> 00:06:01,960
A feeling with no job.
Does it do anything?

107
00:06:01,980 --> 00:06:05,060
Hold a phone number in your
head by repeating it silently.

108
00:06:05,080 --> 00:06:09,820
Now someone makes you say an unrelated word
out loud, again and again, while you try

109
00:06:09,840 --> 00:06:10,620
to keep it.

110
00:06:10,640 --> 00:06:12,050
The number starts to slip.

111
00:06:12,220 --> 00:06:16,280
That's called articulatory suppression,
and it can disrupt some kinds of verbal

112
00:06:16,300 --> 00:06:19,280
memory. It does something similar
to switching between tasks.

113
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:25,140
In experiments by Emerson and Miyake,
people alternating between, say, math and

114
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:29,050
color got slower at switching when
their inner speech was disrupted.

115
00:06:29,220 --> 00:06:32,370
Their reading was that people
silently cue themselves.

116
00:06:32,390 --> 00:06:33,510
Next, color.

117
00:06:33,820 --> 00:06:35,450
So the voice looks like a tool.

118
00:06:35,620 --> 00:06:38,930
A 2024 study complicated
that. Two researchers,

119
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:42,730
Johanne Nedergaard and Gary
Lupyan, went the other direction.

120
00:06:42,750 --> 00:06:46,770
Instead of disrupting the voice, they found
people who report having little of it.

121
00:06:46,790 --> 00:06:51,590
46 adults with low inner speech,
compared with 47 with a lot.

122
00:06:51,660 --> 00:06:54,410
The low group did worse
at verbal working memory.

123
00:06:54,430 --> 00:06:58,000
They were also slower and less accurate
at deciding whether the names of two

124
00:06:58,020 --> 00:07:02,210
pictured objects rhyme. Exactly what
the voice-as-tool idea predicts.

125
00:07:02,480 --> 00:07:06,390
But on task switching, the very thing the
voice is supposed to help with, there was

126
00:07:06,410 --> 00:07:09,430
no difference. Two findings, both real.

127
00:07:09,660 --> 00:07:11,930
Disturb the voice, and
switching gets worse.

128
00:07:12,200 --> 00:07:14,580
Live with little voice, and
switching looks no different.

129
00:07:14,600 --> 00:07:17,380
Nobody knows yet how both can be true.

130
00:07:17,400 --> 00:07:22,540
Suppression isn't a clean off switch, and
a 2026 review stresses how uncertain it

131
00:07:22,560 --> 00:07:25,410
still is what those
experiments actually disrupt.

132
00:07:25,720 --> 00:07:29,600
A mind with less voice might also be doing
the same job another way, though that's a

133
00:07:29,620 --> 00:07:31,640
possibility the evidence doesn't settle.

134
00:07:31,660 --> 00:07:35,960
The authors named the low end
anendophasia, a lack of inner speech.

135
00:07:35,980 --> 00:07:39,110
But critics say the data don't
show anyone has none at all.

136
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:44,560
A 2025 commentary found no compelling
evidence for complete absence, and a 2026 one

137
00:07:44,580 --> 00:07:47,330
took up how absence could
ever be established.

138
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:51,880
It's a fair worry. A few random moments
can't prove a mind never does something.

139
00:07:51,900 --> 00:07:55,400
So "do you have a voice?" turns
out to be the wrong question.

140
00:07:55,420 --> 00:07:56,260
Too crude.

141
00:07:56,280 --> 00:08:00,940
The better one is what language is doing
inside a mind, and what a mind does when it

142
00:08:00,960 --> 00:08:02,340
doesn't use it that way.

143
00:08:02,370 --> 00:08:05,940
The best-supported picture so
far is that the voice is a tool.

144
00:08:06,400 --> 00:08:09,720
Language, built to carry messages
between people, turned inward.

145
00:08:09,740 --> 00:08:13,680
Used to hold a number, to rehearse a
conversation that hasn't happened, to give

146
00:08:13,700 --> 00:08:17,550
yourself an instruction, to imagine
how someone else would answer.

147
00:08:17,570 --> 00:08:19,690
Some minds reach for it constantly.

148
00:08:19,710 --> 00:08:20,710
Some rarely.

149
00:08:20,780 --> 00:08:24,860
And no one has a single theory that
explains every form of it, because "inner

150
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:30,400
speech" isn't one thing. What no one has
fully explained is the feeling itself: why

151
00:08:30,420 --> 00:08:32,720
any of this is experienced as hearing.

152
00:08:32,740 --> 00:08:36,140
Which is where this stops being about
the brain and starts being about you.

153
00:08:36,160 --> 00:08:39,670
You've lived your whole life
inside exactly one mind.

154
00:08:39,690 --> 00:08:41,850
Of course you assumed
it was the average one.

155
00:08:41,920 --> 00:08:44,790
The mistake isn't believing
something false about neuroscience.

156
00:08:44,960 --> 00:08:47,900
It's assuming the person next
to you hears what you hear.

157
00:08:47,920 --> 00:08:52,360
So try one move. The next time you catch
yourself mid-thought, ask what was actually

158
00:08:52,380 --> 00:08:55,250
there a second ago. Words? A picture?

159
00:08:55,270 --> 00:08:57,970
A feeling? Not your habit. That moment.

160
00:08:58,300 --> 00:09:02,280
Researchers lean on moments over summaries
because summaries tend to run high.

161
00:09:02,300 --> 00:09:06,020
And when you talk to someone whose
mind seems foreign to yours, ask them about

162
00:09:06,040 --> 00:09:08,550
a moment too. Not "do you hear a voice?"

163
00:09:08,720 --> 00:09:10,940
but "what was in your head just then?"

164
00:09:10,960 --> 00:09:13,820
Four words, thought, never spoken.

165
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:17,700
A computer, listening to the part of the
brain that prepares speech, recognized

166
00:09:17,720 --> 00:09:18,320
them.

167
00:09:18,340 --> 00:09:19,820
There was no sound to catch.

168
00:09:19,840 --> 00:09:22,820
What it found was language
with the sound taken out.

169
00:09:22,840 --> 00:09:25,400
Language was built to
travel between minds.

170
00:09:25,420 --> 00:09:27,900
In yours, it also learned to stay home.

171
00:09:28,300 --> 00:09:30,480
The sources for this video
are in the description.

172
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:34,180
And if you enjoyed the video, hit that like
button and subscribe — I'll see you in

173
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:35,200
the next one.
